 ### [GEMM-GS Blending](15814-gemm-gs-blending.md)
-GEMM-GS is a rendering optimization for 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) that reformulates the opacity computation into a General Matrix Multiplication (GEMM) compatible dot-product form to leverage GPU Tensor Cores.
+GEMM-GS is a rendering optimization for 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) that reformulates the opacity computation into a General Matrix Multiplication (GEMM) compatible dot-product form to leverage GPU Tensor Cores. Unlike [Warp-Level Culling for Efficient Blending in 3D Gaussian Splatting](12278-warp-level-culling-for-efficient-blending-in-3d-gaussian-spl.md), which focuses on geometric culling to reduce the number of blending operations, GEMM-GS accelerates the remaining blending computations themselves. Choose it when Tensor Core utilization is the bottleneck rather than memory bandwidth or culling overhead.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Performance:** On NVIDIA RTX 4090, the method is up to 19% faster than Faster-GS. On NVIDIA RTX 2070 Super, the method is up to 32% faster. Across all tested GPUs (RTX 4090, RTX 3090, RTX 2070 Super), the changes reduce frame time over Faster-GS by 17% (+20% FPS) on average (geometric mean). On the RTX 2070 Super specifically, the frame time reduction is up to 24% (+32% FPS) [S1].
-*   **Complexity:** The baseline alpha-blending GPU kernel (Faster-GS) performs two to three times slower than the optimistic theoretical bound. This method reduces that gap by eliminating redundant computations [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Maintains identical visual fidelity and differentiability as the original 3DGS implementation. It stays within the CUDA differentiable framework, unlike methods such as PUP 3D-GS, Sort-Free Gaussians, or VKRayGS which sacrifice image quality or differentiability [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires modification of the standard CUDA pipeline. Performance gains are hardware-dependent (tested on NVIDIA RTX 4090, 3090, 2070 Super) [S1].
